          %h3#TopologicalLocalLagrangianGaugeFieldTheory Topological local Lagrangian gauge field theory
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+fundamental+physics+-+table"} standard model of fundamental physics
            is a specification of the general framework of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
          %ol
            %li
              a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
              theory;
            %li
              a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge
              theory;
            %li
              a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
              theory;
            %li
              a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local
              theory;
            %li
              a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological
              theory.
          %p We briefly indicate what this means, a formal discussion of these notions will be given in the main part below.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                theory identifies the basic configurations of a physical system with generalized functions on spacetime, such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+fields"} tensor fields
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                of some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
                over spacetime
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi E
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mi ϕ
                              %mo ↗
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi field
                                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                    %mi bundle
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mi id
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      \&amp;&amp; E
                      \\\
                      \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\phi}}{\nearrow} &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{field\;bundle}}
                      \\\
                      X &amp;\stackrel{id}{\to}&amp; X
                      }
              %p
                (This is the traditional formulation. Below we discuss that the concept of
                %em
                  field
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
                needs to be refined to that of field
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} fiber ∞-bundle
                in order to accuratly capture all aspects of field theory:
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#IdeaOfFieldBundlesAndItsProblems"} The traditional idea of field bundles and its problems
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge
                theory in the broad sense has not just a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
                of field configurations but has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                or
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoid"} smooth groupoid
                of field configurations: there are equivalences between nominally different field configurations
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ≃
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\phi \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \phi'
              %p
                called
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
              %p
                A gauge theory in the original sense specifically has a groupoid of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"} principal connections
                as its configurations. (Notice that gauge transformations are not in general just a redundancy of the description of the theory, but are genuine data: there are in general non-trivial gauge
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
                of a field and the stack of gauge field configurations is not in general equivalent to a plain
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
                theory is one which is specified by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
                on the space of all its configurations, called the (exponentiated)
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi exp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integral
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                that itself is a function of the field configurations and called the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
                of the theory.
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo )
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi x
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\exp(i S(\phi)) = \exp\left(i \int_X L\left(\phi\right)\left(x\right) \right)
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+locus"} critical locus
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Fields
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} var
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi ϕ
                        %mo ≃
                        %mn 0
                        %mo }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\left\{
                      \\phi \in \mathbf{Fields}(\Sigma)		|
                      d_{\mathrm{var}} S_\phi \simeq 0
                      \\right\}
              %p
                of the action functional is called the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                of the theory, a point of this is said to be a solution to the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
                of the theory.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local
                theory has a Lagrangian form which at each point of spacetime only depends on the values of the fields at that point, and of finitely many derivatives of the fields at that point, hence a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mi x
                            %mo )
                          %mo ,
                          %mo ∇
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mi x
                            %mo )
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\exp(i S(\phi)) = \exp\left(i \int_X L\left(\phi\left(x\right), \nabla \phi\left(x\right)\right)\right)
              %p
                This means that a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
                is a horizontal form on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
                of the field bundle of which the fields are sections.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological
                theory finally is one where a field bundle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_X \to X
                is naturally associated to every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
                such as an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metrics"} Riemannian metrics
                on spacetime are themselves regarded as parts of the field content in a topological theory.
          %h3#ChernSimonsTheoryAsAToyExample Chern-Simons field theory as a toy example
          %p
            Most of this was fairly well understood decades ago, by the 1960s. But even with all this conceptual understanding of the structure of fundamental physics available, there were and are a lot of implications of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field+theory"} gauge field theory
            which remained elusive.
          %p
            In the 1980s attention turned to
            %em other
            topological local Lagrangian gauge field theories than that governing the standard model: it turns out that there are many such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories+of+physics"} theories of physics
            that share crucial properties with the standard model but which otherwise predict, if considered as phenomenological models, physics radically different from that which we actually observe experimentally. For instance there are respectable theories of fundamental physics which describe physics in dimensions other than the three spatial and one time dimension which we clearly observe, theories that contain no force of gravity, others that contain only the force of gravity, theories that contain mirror partners of every species of matter or contain no matter at all, etc.
          %p In short, it was gradually realized, and realized to be important, that, in the platonic world of mathematics and of theoretical physics, there is a whole space of field theories, in which the one underlying the standard model of observed physics is only one single point, even if regarded with its free parameters.
          %p
            A crucial step forward happened when around 1989
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            a topological local Lagrangian gauge field theory which, as such, shares some crucial principles with Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills theory, while at the same time being radically different and most importantly: much simpler to analyze.
          %p
            The dimension of Chern-Simons theory is 3 instead of 4, but Chern-Simons theory has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            just as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            does. For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+algebroid-valued+differential+form"} Lie algebra-valued differential 1-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+3-form"} Chern-Simons 3-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CS
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L(A)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\mathrm{CS}_3(A)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\langle A \wedge F_A\rangle
                  +
                  c
                  \\langle A \wedge [A \wedge A]\rangle
                  \\,,
          %p
            whence the name of the theory, and hence the Chern-Simons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CS
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i S(A))
                  \=
                  \\exp\left(
                  2 \pi i \int_{\Sigma_X} \mathrm{CS}_3(A)
                  \\right)
          %p
            At the same time Chern-Simons theory is still being rich in phenomena, in fact so rich in phenomena that for instance it helped illuminate deep mathematical problems that had not otherwise been understood as much before (properties called
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariants"} knot invariants
          %h3#TopologicalLocalFieldTheoryInHigherGeometry Topological local field theory in higher category theory
          %p
            Another pleasant effect of the introduction of the Chern-Simons “toy model” for topological field theory was that, due to its conceptual simplicity, its basic structure could be appreciated also by mathematicians not trained in theoretical physics, who would otherwise fail to see through all the physics jargon involved in the available discussion of realistic models. Accordingly, immediately after the realization of Chern-Simons field theory, the first mathematically precise axiomatizations of an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theory"} topological field theory
            was proposed, by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"} Michael Atiyah
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+quantum+field+theory"} functorial quantum field theory
          %p
            This axiomatization demands in particular that to each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            is assigned a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\Sigma_{n-1} \mapsto Z(\Sigma_{n-1}) \in \mathrm{Vect}
          %p
            thought of as the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+physical+states"} space of physical states
            of the theory on the spatial slice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n-1}
            of spacetime.
          %p
            However, This original axiomatization did not capture the full
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} locality
            property: the spaces of states are assigned globally to a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n-1}
            and are not required to be obtainable by “integrating up local data” over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n-1}
          %p
            In order to refine this, one needs an axiomatization of field theory that assigns data also to manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{k}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \leq n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi k
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{k} \times S^1
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            in a suitable sense, of the data assigned to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{k}
            itself. It turns out that the way to capture this is to think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(\Sigma_k)
            as being a vector space in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory"} directed homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} (n-k)-vector space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi k
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma_k \mapsto Z(\Sigma_k) \in (n-k)\mathrm{Vect}
          %p
            That something like this should work was hypothetized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Dan Freed
            (in
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebraic+Structures+and+Quantization"} Higher Algebraic Structures and Quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            (in
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+field+theory"} extended topological field theory
            (with “extended” referring to extending the axioms from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            1 to higher codimension) or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-tiered+field+theory"} multi-tiered field theory
            (thinking of the data in higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            as being higher “tiers” of the theory).
          %p
            But in the
            = succeed "-community," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "-community," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            which refers broadly to what the axioms of “extended TQFT” capture. Hence we should think of this as formalizing
            = succeed "." do
              %em topological local field theory
          %h3#TopologcalLocalLagrangianFieldTheoryInHigherGeometry
            Topological local
            %em Lagrangian
            field theory in higher geometry
          %p
            The formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+field+theory"} extended topological field theory
            thus captures the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local
            aspect of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            %a{:href => "#TopologicalLocalLagrangianGaugeFieldTheory"} above
            that the theories of fundamental physics have one more crucial property: they are
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+field+theory"} extended field theory
          %p
            The step of passing from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            to the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} algebraic deformation quantization
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+quantum+observables"} algebras of quantum observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            that we considered above. Both are at least roughly dual to each other, in the spirit of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} the general duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
          %div
