                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                  \\to
                  \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The nontrivial part is that, as one can see, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            refines to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} moduli ∞-stacks
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stackification"} ∞-stackification
            of what is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Deligne+cup+product"} Beilinson-Deligne cup product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi c
                      %mo ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi c
                      %mo ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat \mathbf{c}_1 \cup \widehat \mathbf{c}_1
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                  \\to
                  \\mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"} extended Lagrangian
            for abelian Chern-Simons theory. On field configuration which happens to be given by a globally defined 1-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            its value is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 3
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %mi A
                    %mo )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp\left(
                  2\pi i \int_{\Sigma_3} A \wedge d A
                  \\right)
                  \\in
                  U(1)
                  \\,.
          %p But now not every field configuration is necessarily given by a globally defined 1-form anymore, and more generally the action functional has a more sophisticated expression.
          %h3#7dCSTheoryAsExample 7-dimensional Chern-Simons theory as the next example
          %p
            So far we have considered general motivation for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            naturally captures the characteristics of the theory. Now we consider further examples whose higher geometry has not necessarily been considered traditionally.
          %p
            An immediate class of further examples is obtained by abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher Chern-Simons theory
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
            with itself
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi DD
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mi DD
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [DD_{2k} \cup DD_{2k}] \in H^{4(k+1)}(B^{2k+1} U(1), \mathbb{Z})
                  \\,.
          %p
            This has a refinement (
            %a{:href => "#FSSCup"}> FSS cup
            ) to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+characteristic+class"} differential characteristic class
            by the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Deligne+cup+product"} Beilinson-Deligne cup product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+characteristic+class"} differential refinement
            of the higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dixmier-Douady+class"} Dixmier-Douady class
            with itself:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi DD
                      %mo ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi DD
                      %mo ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat \mathbf{DD}_{2k}
                  \\cup
                  \\widehat \mathbf{DD}_{2k}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{B}^{2k+1} U(1)_{conn}
                  \\to
                  \\mathbf{B}^{4k+3} U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In particular there is the abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi DD
                      %mo ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi DD
                      %mo ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 7
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat \mathbf{DD}_2
                  \\cup
                  \\widehat \mathbf{DD}_2
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}
                  \\to
                  \\mathbf{B}^7 U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            An argument in (
            %a{:href => "#AdS-CFT#Witten98"}> Witten 98
            ) says that this abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
            (or more precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            of it) is related to the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            sector of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-supersymmetric+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-supersymmetric QFT
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            of a single
            = succeed "_" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            is related to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theory"} ∞-Chern-Simons theory
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ←
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftarrow
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightarrow
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory"} ∞-Wess-Zumino-Witten theory
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+reduction"} Kaluza-Klein reduction
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                %th
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a{:href => "holographic+principle#3dCS-2dCFT"} 3dCS-2dCFT
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a{:href => "AdS-CFT#AdS7CFT6"} AdS7/CFT6 duality
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-supersymmetric+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-supersymmetric QFT
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D4+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=4 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            The argument in (
            %a{:href => "#AdS-CFT#Witten98"}> Witten 98
            ) also says that in view of
            %a{:href => "AdS-CFT#AdS7CFT6"} AdS7/CFT6 duality
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            of the 7d theory here is to be identified withe the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %p
            But the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            is known to have moduli that are in general more complicated than just
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}
            (due to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+anomaly+cancellation"} Green-Schwarz anomaly cancellation
            mechanism in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            and a “flux quantization” condition). In (
            %a{:href => "#FSS7dCS"}> FSS 7dCD
            ) the corrected moduli are dicussed and found to involve a coupling of the abelian 7d theory with a nonabelian 7d Chern-Simons theory whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            are twisted higher spin connections: “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+string+structures"}> twisted differential string structures
            ”.
          %p
            To see what this is, recall that
            %a{:href => "#CSAsArchetypicalExample"} above
            we interpreted the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"} extended Lagrangian
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            for a simply connected compact simple gauge group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as the smooth and moreover differential refinement of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+characteristic+class"} universal characteristic class
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^4(B G, \mathbb{Z})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            this is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+fractional+Pontryagin+class"} first fractional Pontryagin class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+map"} characteristic map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tfrac{1}{2}p_1 \;\colon\; B Spin \to B^3 U(1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This can be understood as a higher analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Stiefel-Whitney+class"} second Stiefel-Whitney class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mi SO
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w_2 \;\colon\; B SO \to B^2 \mathbb{Z}
            which is the universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tfrac{1}{2}p_1
            is the universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
            to a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+spin+structure"} higher spin structure
            called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            (this and the motivation for this terminology is discussed in detail in
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "geometry+pf+physics##FieldsOfGravityAndGeneralizedGeometry"} geometry of physics - Fields - Examples - Fields of gravity and G-structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-invariants"} k-invariants
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B O
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-group"} smooth
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
                    of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} moduli ∞-stacks
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"}> G-structure
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+spin+structure"} higher spin structure
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math#mathml_a3e36bd258145a21573b883b370a878050ce09e8_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ⋮
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdots
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math#mathml_a3e36bd258145a21573b883b370a878050ce09e8_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \downarrow
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ninebrane+10-group"} ninebrane 10-group
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math#mathml_a3e36bd258145a21573b883b370a878050ce09e8_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi Ninebrane
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}Ninebrane
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ninebrane+structure"} ninebrane structure
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/third+fractional+Pontryagin+class"} third fractional Pontryagin class
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math#mathml_a3e36bd258145a21573b883b370a878050ce09e8_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \downarrow
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
